#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129269 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128846 :question/id #uuid "b22d04e0-8c6a-507a-9d76-ab05adb32b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11517" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $\\mathbf{T}:(u,v,w) \\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation $\\mathbf T^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to(u(x,y,z), v(x,y,z), w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n![](images/f8/f82cf760dee34626c290e29420fbaddb4ef254180087d05bb6fb883f2dc32e75.svg)\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x>0$, $y \\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/1c/1c2567cd1c2cf28ed17b6dc445025cb60061dc7d7d6c78c960be77f8866ef4a7.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z) = x^2, \\qquad \nv(x,y,z) = xy, \\qquad\nw(x,y,z) = yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 0 & 0 \\\\ y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x|\\begin{aligned}x & 0 \\\\ z & y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2x(xy-0) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y & =2\\cdot x\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\sqrt{u}\\cdot v \\\\  & =2v\\sqrt{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2v\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048251 :topic/math-academy-id 4149 :topic/title "The Inverse Function Theorem"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "408e29c9-6522-5467-9ab8-def9fe8cfb65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324490} {:answer/id #uuid "69224e59-e1c5-54cd-8748-b2d597b5cd76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{w}{u}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324848} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1e869c-a3b7-584b-8e0f-e99423947eb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325004} {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5ec600-18ee-54e3-bb28-7c22c6783223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e98f16dc-8225-5762-aa80-dca5e80b6645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326027} :answer-field/id #uuid "65802821-8118-5ece-8435-a69816ad88f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326394}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f85af4c0-4520-5614-952b-cb9d6d06c6c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39300" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}+z \\\\ {y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0$ and $y>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{y}+z,\\,z(x,y,z)={y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}} & 1 \\\\ 2y & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}} & =(\\frac{1}{x})^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{w} \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}\\sqrt{w}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a3dfa61-4983-5101-a590-b7cae3069606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324062} {:answer/id #uuid "5975b6e2-4d63-5522-bc15-85311b4304c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324714} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d3c1d8-1ca4-5893-ae40-8a4995898733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325383} {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb623d2-5638-5465-9671-82a386df23f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5fd3b47-8f20-518a-a996-2dbd4fa2edc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325679} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d44cd8a-7ab2-5c59-a740-4c19610ad44c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326377}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9ad6bc33-7c9a-5d03-8204-18b81f486eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-39669" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=-x+3y-2z,\\,w=3y+4z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(12+6)-1(-4-0) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d889e7-c824-5206-ae1f-be2ca84ac201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278809} {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2bc2ed-1ff1-5cb1-beed-8d5a3ea43e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280124} {:answer/id #uuid "b815cee2-db42-5d6f-beef-5073c80b1726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280211} {:answer/id #uuid "feaf148b-7dd0-5012-95f0-0e1627a40f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54eae97a-126b-5169-968a-945a94267a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279365} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2c215b-9be4-534d-b695-b69d79a8eec9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280877}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "867c4c3a-9ff3-5bda-af78-c2d51483fcb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b82b5c-a4bc-5662-ada6-dda7518ac8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278655} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} {:answer/id #uuid "413c7a3a-e6cb-5e3d-b351-58d589d29f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279171} {:answer/id #uuid "7cce492a-e940-5ea6-a555-ee7f7af4f9ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279706} {:answer/id #uuid "ffff14cf-d01e-51fb-a239-eecaf7d789e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc85f22-4a58-5c43-9914-7b18675f3668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278731} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd07e6bc-736c-579e-90c2-ab826af79391" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281217}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9050d05-b087-5029-bf1c-8747b808c301" :question/math-academy-id "q-161800" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=z-y,\\,v=x+z,\\,w=-3x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =-3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0+3)+1(0-0) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c2caa68-f00a-5537-8bd9-f6cb76c877ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279273} {:answer/id #uuid "600dfa31-4e37-5a16-ba48-c5ca10f9fbf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279473} {:answer/id #uuid "b204a652-49ca-5415-ba78-58814749d96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280165} {:answer/id #uuid "c706e56c-9384-5437-a653-797286bbede3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280336} {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa5dc6c7-df6e-5fdb-b0fa-59f45de8fd17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280789} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d4e8c1-df31-56ff-9384-be31853aa6b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280891}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f0a4eb6-83e4-58b7-81d7-e22ca020da04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161801" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=y-3z,\\,v=x+2z,\\,w=3x-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1(0-6)-3(-1-0) \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6cc79b-3207-598f-8d70-80b25ee5e718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278846} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d99844-e42f-56e8-86a6-5d5458f5fa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280045} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c82ac3-d2d1-560d-a1c4-451dfa40fa91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280052} {:answer/id #uuid "cc93804e-fbe9-508c-968d-dea27a808ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280388} {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f373368b-6b80-5e55-91f1-61c0a4bf9baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280720} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff6d4ed4-6f38-58ef-90e7-56012b55823a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78cdb0a8-90d4-5ce3-9ce4-dfaf056d437e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161802" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=3x+yz,\\,v=z,\\,w=-y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & z & y \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(0+1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281334 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b44cea0-ad33-5976-923f-1f474b30b292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278975} {:answer/id #uuid "460aadea-67c4-543b-8250-5f0974161a89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279217} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4a44db-5da9-5c22-8623-1c9463ab6122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279447} {:answer/id #uuid "b93d3489-33b8-59f0-b4e7-ed465bfeb6ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u+v+w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280221} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebb87757-2b77-5b42-9577-c7fa19032c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280664} :answer-field/id #uuid "00162f26-ff18-503b-9399-9d89f9b3560f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280834}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "256171d6-afaa-51cd-ae04-b8a4f7e24f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-161804" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} \\\\ {e}^{x+y} \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)={e}^{x},\\,v(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y},\\,w(x,y,z)={e}^{x+y+z}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & ={e}^{x} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y} & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & ={e}^{x+y+z} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & ={e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y} & {e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y} & 0 \\\\ {e}^{x+y+z} & {e}^{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({e}^{2x+2y+z}-0) \\\\  & ={e}^{3x+2y+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+2y+z} & ={e}^{x+(x+y)+(x+y+z)} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot {e}^{x+y+z} \\\\  & =uvw\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} {:answer/id #uuid "47b2751a-712d-5998-bd6d-e5e60e8c5b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{v+w}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279233} {:answer/id #uuid "7a98c4b3-ed46-5cfd-8a6c-e55f7a475d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(w-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279683} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa88b3c-e8a4-5165-97f7-0e1e8c3eec3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279962} {:answer/id #uuid "f792ff52-2762-5037-adc2-ea50c01f1006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d44b6cc-3a93-5117-a27f-6f280b9b11c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278862} :answer-field/id #uuid "db389b2e-8734-58ef-b276-82b4a342763c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281214}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21d2a60a-f1f7-5e79-815f-2e9babe829a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161805" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ z-y \\\\ xy\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=x,\\,v(x,y,z)=z-y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xy.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ y & x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ x & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-x) \\\\  & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21988933-2fdd-5343-979b-14ba2f6e68d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278894} {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} {:answer/id #uuid "4e964f3b-c23d-5355-8277-f886ef034e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279306} {:answer/id #uuid "7bc53c83-70d3-5bd8-adc4-31458e829c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4uvw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279694} {:answer/id #uuid "8888de73-401c-58c0-8c74-d084f5008000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{uvw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "413d8651-c816-595c-bfd1-f51fa29900be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279172} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb87408-7532-5806-bc11-5c1c98d338e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281257}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9bd3e29c-50b2-5640-b3fa-d0adf89e1fac" :question/math-academy-id "q-161806" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}xy \\\\ yz \\\\ xz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z>0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=xy,\\,v(x,y,z)=yz,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =y & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =x & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =y \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y|\\begin{aligned}z & y \\\\ 0 & x\\end{aligned}|-x|\\begin{aligned}0 & y \\\\ z & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =y(xz-0)-x(0-yz) \\\\  & =xyz+xyz \\\\  & =2xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xyz & =2\\sqrt{(xyz)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{xy(yz)(xz)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{uvw}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{uvw}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0626020c-ffd0-5d52-be60-af389fc83a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{1/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278648} {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} {:answer/id #uuid "84467733-f429-5feb-8388-07e2d51f176e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(uvw)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279778} {:answer/id #uuid "af416bf2-0b93-5782-b6de-1f1c2d402fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(uvw)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280143} {:answer/id #uuid "c8f31297-6e1d-54c7-be43-6fdd25ab29fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{(uvw)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bde7fd-ba02-508b-9c77-edf62367e8ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbffbbf-30e0-555b-b5e7-55199b3bfffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280913}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "82aa7a5f-aede-5ec9-8984-8cdea4eb6f95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161807" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y{z}^{2} \\\\ z{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $x,y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=y{z}^{2},\\,v(x,y,z)=z{x}^{2},\\,w(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & ={z}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2yz \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =2xz & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & ={y}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =2xy & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & {z}^{2} & 2yz \\\\ 2xz & 0 & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 0\\end{aligned}|+2yz|\\begin{aligned}2xz & 0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-{z}^{2}(0-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})+2yz(4{x}^{2}yz-0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}{z}^{2} & =9{({x}^{3}{y}^{3}{z}^{3})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9{(y{z}^{2}\\cdot z{x}^{2}\\cdot x{y}^{2})}^{2/3} \\\\  & =9(uvw)^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9(uvw)^{2/3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7a65b8-5911-5995-890e-26eb26de2d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{vw}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424166} {:answer/id #uuid "cda476b6-e024-5c13-a813-a0217e4446ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424167} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf34300-0330-5f0e-9e12-24a74c4b83db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2u}{v-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424170} {:answer/id #uuid "fa885cf6-9f29-528c-8aeb-86e960fc0083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{w}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba467583-3a17-5baa-8344-2ff452a1d080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{u}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424165} :answer-field/id #uuid "f602c505-c9b2-58a6-873a-3101dc02439a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bbf87425-cede-5e7e-870f-278bd2b11904" :question/math-academy-id "q-161808" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)).$ The inverse transformation ${T}^{-1}(x,y,z)\\to (u(x,y,z),v(x,y,z),w(x,y,z))$ is given by\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{y} \\\\ y \\\\ xz\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the Jacobian determinant of $T.$\n\n*Hint: You can assume that $y,z\\ne 0.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nu(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{y},\\,v(x,y,z)=y,\\,w(x,y,z)=xz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =z & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =z|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =z(0-\\frac{1}{y}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting down $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}$ in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z}{y} & =-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-u)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0584ff70-04f9-53f4-b918-d40c95661794" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424148} {:answer/id #uuid "274585a0-b8c0-5396-9ba1-f844d74ca493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424151} {:answer/id #uuid "2dad3fa0-8b4c-595d-a450-31167f619d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424152} {:answer/id #uuid "d83940ba-986b-5a11-adf3-d93e8cc8e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} {:answer/id #uuid "f587a66a-7aeb-56f0-8880-52bcb09867b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83940ba-986b-5a11-adf3-d93e8cc8e5b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9faf9fb-252c-510a-b14d-4675e0404650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424175}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa96e0b4-9142-5b9e-b173-68571a2c2de3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161797" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y,\\,w=3z.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(6-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129270 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128847 :question/id #uuid "b16845a2-5f66-524a-90ea-37f8a8186f8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-11518" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ where $\\mathbf T$ is defined as\n\n![](images/22/22690753019e406703952ae0c2a79450764b5ff42c758a03b2875a9d428d9e78.svg)" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/be/be4c52a967cf6430f1d365ebb9b3a3a20bc852ac6422ed51e01dc5b39055c816.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w) =w-u^3, \\qquad \ny(u,v,w) = 2uv, \\qquad\nz(u,v,w) = u^2+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-3{u}^{2} & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 2u & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 2u \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3{u}^{2}(0-0)+1(2v-4{u}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(v-2{u}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} =0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2(v-2{u}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ v-2{u}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-2u^2 = 0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $v=2u^2.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b4a31c6-01be-5b15-88f5-9150f350e944" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050005 :topic/math-academy-id 1898 :topic/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ef2524-c316-5f5c-bb17-9410d6df7855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = 2v$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126656} {:answer/id #uuid "af21f9b2-947b-5b4b-9b83-b2324aabe6c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u =-w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126657} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ef1406-0abe-5cfe-bc04-7ae04a77ecf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u = w$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126658} {:answer/id #uuid "e90dd075-4fb2-51bb-9367-44b42c15619d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw -$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126659} {:answer/id #uuid "0cffeeef-f100-51b5-ac9e-aa76adbf7807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u = 2w^{2}$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126660}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4d271abc-b1d9-5284-9062-68834149c800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126655} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59ecd82e-c85b-5221-81f4-5aa437d23a7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401290} {:answer/id #uuid "1680c5f5-1c51-52cd-a03a-ad30d3c07c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401291} {:answer/id #uuid "92c1139b-4ea6-5a6e-91ff-1cd1ee2688f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401292} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401293} {:answer/id #uuid "b1928d32-8a83-5bc0-a68f-3b8d2a17bd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea440aa-4bbc-5828-a750-3ec0ce76a35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401293} :answer-field/id #uuid "17caa721-6c88-5948-86f1-24aa0f77f9b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401289}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "685e989d-8721-5a8c-a4d8-0e44efe4bc9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161819" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-{w}^{2} \\\\ w{e}^{u} \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-{w}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u},\\,z(u,v,w)=u-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =-2w \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 & -2w \\\\ w{e}^{u} & 0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u} \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}|-2w|\\begin{aligned}w{e}^{u} & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(0+{e}^{u})-2w(-w{e}^{u}-0) \\\\  & =2u{e}^{u}+2{w}^{2}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2{e}^{u}({w}^{2}+u) & =0 \\\\ {w}^{2}+u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${w}^{2}+u=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $u=-{w}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} {:answer/id #uuid "06b7dea6-5d67-5db6-9562-564a467e6927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402684} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdabe0b-331f-59b0-be6a-e0b333cf7d10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder ${w}^{2}=2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402685} {:answer/id #uuid "edca51bc-5f25-52b9-81ee-5e945aaea583" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402686} {:answer/id #uuid "2daa5374-5e80-5f12-9e7f-d5612c496bb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f2fafa-94fa-537a-9de2-cde9b153270f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402683} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec45c948-28d5-5ad6-a116-f2bf6a3a31b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402682}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab44c8de-700d-51cc-ab4a-3755f7926aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-39709" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ {u}^{2}+v \\\\ {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}-v,\\,y(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+v,\\,z(u,v,w)={e}^{w}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{w}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {e}^{w}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}|\\begin{aligned}2u & -1 \\\\ 2u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(2u+2u) \\\\  & =4u{e}^{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 4u{e}^{w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281338 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b1e167f-c36b-51bb-8f7d-1c5abfba9d3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{u}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278842} {:answer/id #uuid "398d98c4-80d9-5695-9ad1-7904a7156b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279097} {:answer/id #uuid "50abbce6-3f16-512e-a087-34dc285e7cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279329} {:answer/id #uuid "cc12c5cd-4583-53a4-9f13-8cababa0d611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280382} {:answer/id #uuid "de43abc5-071a-5046-a899-84beee7c637b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50abbce6-3f16-512e-a087-34dc285e7cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279329} :answer-field/id #uuid "73950424-d736-5015-94bb-837d8bf513c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281040}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d47d2404-bd86-5706-ba43-4f015172617a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161809" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}uv \\\\ u+v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=uv,\\,y(u,v,w)=u+v,\\,z(u,v,w)=w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}v & u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}v & u \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ v-u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-u=0$ defines the plane $v=u..$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710d7d7d-97b4-592f-b30c-acf388d38859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279617} {:answer/id #uuid "76ca4acf-773c-501c-ba6e-25239ada0435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279651} {:answer/id #uuid "7b363e77-7f40-535f-a3a7-6bd580690682" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279692} {:answer/id #uuid "88ce4141-06f6-5738-b4cf-0fb6b9a3a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279813} {:answer/id #uuid "eaed37b4-3321-5a47-9faa-28666138fd1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88ce4141-06f6-5738-b4cf-0fb6b9a3a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279813} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf9576c-0ce8-5723-bb46-cfba69aeb672" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281170}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f26bca76-37a5-5f68-8e4a-4d9215b02535" :question/math-academy-id "q-161812" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ {v}^{3}+{w}^{3} \\\\ v+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{3}+{w}^{3},\\,z(u,v,w)=v+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3{v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3{w}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}3{v}^{2} & 3{w}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{v}^{2}-3{w}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3({v}^{2}-{w}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2}-{w}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation ${v}^{2}-{w}^{2}=0$ defines a pair of intersecting planes $v=w$ and $v=-w.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e5c045a-d984-5c99-8fa3-0129ac115c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278721} {:answer/id #uuid "2d089752-d1ca-57d4-aaad-39bd60fd867f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278988} {:answer/id #uuid "768c6810-e6a9-5693-b6ba-88bee24c33f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} {:answer/id #uuid "9f89b797-8be9-5dae-9e56-f0b7e9d1b9f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280008} {:answer/id #uuid "bc5b203f-69ef-5d8b-b9f1-3986949f0305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "768c6810-e6a9-5693-b6ba-88bee24c33f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279649} :answer-field/id #uuid "55be6ad3-d866-57e2-8467-8385c57eb9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280977}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2d2dfd0-abce-5610-a53e-633420f43575" :question/math-academy-id "q-161815" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{u} \\\\ vw \\\\ u+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\sqrt{u},\\,y(u,v,w)=vw,\\,z(u,v,w)=u+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & w & v \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}|\\begin{aligned}w & v \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}(w-0)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{w}{2\\sqrt{u}} & =0 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w=0$ defines the plane $w=0,$ i.e., $uv$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012e5e71-30c4-576f-9027-84673f0cec11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${u}^{2}+{v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278593} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca3f8ee-ee06-5aa0-b1ee-b1a91215f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278855} {:answer/id #uuid "aedf9c47-b56b-5e71-845a-05c0a9e74384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the cylinder ${v}^{2}+{w}^{2}=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280139} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e3d435-b0b0-5133-a7de-47ee24110c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280736} {:answer/id #uuid "f7242b75-d7a6-5b47-a2a4-a6f542bd4ba3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca3f8ee-ee06-5aa0-b1ee-b1a91215f89a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278855} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0414cad-2a72-50dc-8e82-0bf67299254f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ae1d4bd-a84e-5282-96ec-f8259badfc91" :question/math-academy-id "q-161817" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2u+v+3w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u,\\,y(u,v,w)=v{e}^{w},\\,z(u,v,w)=2u+v+3w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =v{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & v{e}^{w} \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(3{e}^{w}-v{e}^{w}) \\\\  & ={e}^{w}(3-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{w}(3-v) & =0 \\\\ 3-v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-v=0$ defines the plane $v=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0360b3bf-0e11-5644-974a-fb91e4539e1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278610} {:answer/id #uuid "40d23535-8e40-5b39-96da-dc0c5913b017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the planes $v=\\pm w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279167} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e4c43-7095-567c-8087-ac111d00d4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279286} {:answer/id #uuid "a24f7a5b-f9c2-51ab-bfe8-2b6ef54faa12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280034} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca341c-0372-5300-8b3a-24814e46f956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0ca341c-0372-5300-8b3a-24814e46f956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280285} :answer-field/id #uuid "0966bd13-e522-5f74-88dd-66c421ecae33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280850}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5828c68b-3870-5e91-8f73-8351ac68b7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-161818" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+3w \\\\ v+2w \\\\ {v}^{2}-u\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)={v}^{2}-u.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2v & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2v & 0\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4v)+3(0+1) \\\\  & =3-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 3-4v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $3-4v=0$ defines the plane $v=\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b9f18b-ec2f-51d5-ae14-92461f9907ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $wv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376126} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacd98b-62c2-5e98-a210-80793aec69cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376127} {:answer/id #uuid "13bfe028-f756-50dc-a99d-e4bd7e879772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad07193-94a4-5bd2-b02f-54c7365c5656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376129} {:answer/id #uuid "69b5ad50-abba-5674-a016-be9139fe02be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13bfe028-f756-50dc-a99d-e4bd7e879772" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cb516a-a7eb-5761-97af-05385415c8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376125}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a710b40d-30b7-5142-a877-a2aee690f184" :question/math-academy-id "q-161813" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}|\\begin{aligned}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53f14d31-90ea-5326-97dc-45022d0835ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u+v-2w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401277} {:answer/id #uuid "b740b568-485c-5311-a423-33ea2f3eeffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the elliptic cylinder ${u}^{2}+2{w}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401278} {:answer/id #uuid "a6795027-5482-5bff-8214-2dd2cc097fe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401279} {:answer/id #uuid "5adbc1f1-362c-537c-bb50-2cdecc09b123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$ plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401280} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3a89ced-7070-57be-88cf-4d4b894bd3f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $u=2w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401281} :answer-field/id #uuid "65676948-b2fa-585d-8619-ca89f50c6f34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401276}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8752c18-2285-5bd2-a3b2-ba4d0420bd04" :question/math-academy-id "q-161814" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+2w \\\\ v-u \\\\ uw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,y(u,v,w)=v-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=uw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ w & 0 & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & u\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ w & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(u-0)+2(0-w) \\\\  & =u-2w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ u-2w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u-2w=0$ defines the plane $u=2w.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401282 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} {:answer/id #uuid "10a3627e-14c0-553d-8005-b8e65337d0b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=-2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401285} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ffd8d2-f852-57f7-9970-8454309f0281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $2u+v+w=0.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401286} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fe9b5d-4fbe-56d7-9627-c5c1df64a715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401287} {:answer/id #uuid "e3cbb05e-806b-56aa-8136-04b088a6dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w={u}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b76c85a4-c631-5c36-941d-c14613479d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401284} :answer-field/id #uuid "175d9200-8100-5e54-b984-b7451df1967c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401283}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc380b3f-d92e-5c45-8651-fac240787b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161816" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} \\\\ {e}^{u+w} \\\\ uv+w\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={e}^{u+w},\\,z(u,v,w)=uv+w.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & ={e}^{u+w} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & ={e}^{u+w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 0 & 0 \\\\ {e}^{u+w} & 0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ v & u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}0 & {e}^{u+w} \\\\ u & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(0-u{e}^{u+w}) \\\\  & =-u{e}^{2u+w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -u{e}^{2u+w} & =0 \\\\ u & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $u=0$ defines the plane $u=0,$ i.e., $vw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406771 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} {:answer/id #uuid "47f0e73b-3fcc-5922-ac4c-5cbbb15dc078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406762} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd27081-6df8-5fcc-87e8-0b763e12dd24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406763} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd4d236-b060-5654-b754-6a3374d3448b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=1.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406766} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaabaf5-f0e8-54a9-9f5a-d6c4751feaf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=u.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06cfa4e4-4978-5d07-b498-b67163d323ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406760} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a97003-66fd-5b44-944b-7b4ac3d8364b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84951e54-c705-5b01-85eb-275b777e397c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161810" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u+vw \\\\ {v}^{2} \\\\ w+v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u+vw,\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2},\\,z(u,v,w)=w+v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & w & v \\\\ 0 & 2v & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(2v-0) \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ 2v & =0 \\\\ v & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v=0$ defines the plane $v=0,$ i.e., $uw$-plane."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424180 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e37282e-8869-5ad2-9f01-e062a7d21846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $v=2{w}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424153} {:answer/id #uuid "4a31e95b-7336-5699-a485-0b22a7563452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} {:answer/id #uuid "56201c08-96c0-58d8-9ed7-c9a2a6ecea11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $vw-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424156} {:answer/id #uuid "7447254c-742a-5c7f-adb1-193a3168832a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424159} {:answer/id #uuid "7473d403-04c8-5521-92cd-20ba6d10f904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a31e95b-7336-5699-a485-0b22a7563452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $v=w.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424154} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0a37030-5cd9-5e43-a291-e19dfc94c67f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424176}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b50230b8-d517-560a-9ede-747bedde3c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161811" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u}+v \\\\ v+w \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={e}^{u}+v,\\,y(u,v,w)=v+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{u}(v-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{u}(v-w) & =0 \\\\ v-w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $v-w=0$ defines the plane $v=w.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Critical Points of a Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129271 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128848 :question/id #uuid "66d757d2-f2ad-5f0d-b4f1-8d426f4c8351" :question/math-academy-id "e-11579" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ that maps the Cartesian $uvw$-space onto the Cartesian $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx = 2u-v,\\qquad\ny = u+3v+w,\\qquad\nz = u+2v-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n![](images/6e/6e94e780387ccbba3d8b6a6aaf5f00fbf362cd8163837a8ea78ad31c18f3b1b3.svg)\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $\\mathbf T,$ denoted $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} ,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =3 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-3-2)+(-1-1) \\\\  & =-10-2 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d433063f-f145-5bf4-8bd4-8d5e5b36b3d8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219 :topic/math-academy-id 1999 :topic/title "The Jacobian"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048505 :topic/math-academy-id 1770 :topic/title "Finding Determinants Using Laplace Expansions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049159 :topic/math-academy-id 1771 :topic/title "Basic Properties of Determinants"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ec0914-5828-5c4a-81c9-ce52c1488ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323999} {:answer/id #uuid "350992e5-036c-535c-8a4b-6a5ab4c6fa16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324405} {:answer/id #uuid "81e74165-da06-5f93-ae92-aeef9db19857" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325063} {:answer/id #uuid "a528987d-54c7-5615-ae6d-cbc6a9db0c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} {:answer/id #uuid "efd74025-7232-59ef-bfa0-45a2a9dfad28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a528987d-54c7-5615-ae6d-cbc6a9db0c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325376} :answer-field/id #uuid "60477555-dd44-54e5-9698-cd175b7154c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326385}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24830b7e-b0be-5ebb-9a57-2cd58c40cf8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-39299" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +v\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=v\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=w.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $\\theta $ is a fixed constant." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c262fc-9bb7-50e9-9bd9-71220d222eee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323988} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7284f1-bddd-57c4-bee7-4e81f853fddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2vw\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324235} {:answer/id #uuid "45d58fec-b6fd-54bf-819d-1320f8bbad5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324536} {:answer/id #uuid "5a92451e-4ced-5c86-9ad8-ed111fb7bb27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4vw\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324719} {:answer/id #uuid "b55d6eaa-09cc-570c-89c0-c7bfca63934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b55d6eaa-09cc-570c-89c0-c7bfca63934e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325531} :answer-field/id #uuid "785a8a4c-9863-5dc4-b03a-b4c3232d979e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326437}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "045d5f30-3ffc-5bb0-bb67-dd9e010fc71b" :question/math-academy-id "q-39666" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}4v \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw) \\\\ vw\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=4v,\\,y(u,v,w)=3{u}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(vw),\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =6u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =-2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6u & -2w\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}6u & -2v\\operatorname{sin}⁡(vw) \\\\ 0 & v\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(6uv-0) \\\\  & =-24uv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46d3639c-107c-558c-b1c4-6ddd7cb81df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{u}^{3}v-{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279222} {:answer/id #uuid "58a20343-ced5-57fc-b813-e98d7f250092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(3{u}^{2}+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279406} {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ccb9-adf9-523b-b70b-b84e01b6cdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279416} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0b239b-6293-5a6c-a399-4c20fbd9ba89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280232} {:answer/id #uuid "c9d8a949-98c9-53b2-a5c0-d91ecebaaf5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-u{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ccb9-adf9-523b-b70b-b84e01b6cdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{u}^{3}-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279416} :answer-field/id #uuid "095a93fc-962d-5e7a-8222-89b127de9857" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba1e3545-8702-5a08-88b1-f7be09c82e4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161792" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=u{v}^{2}+w,\\,y={u}^{3}+v,\\,z=uv.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={v}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2uv & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =3{u}^{2} & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{v}^{2} & 2uv & 1 \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1 & 0 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a3a5e9-764a-5e8b-8b50-93add8b3fc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279099} {:answer/id #uuid "723714f3-b594-566d-a56e-12b3c635b92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279630} {:answer/id #uuid "790dd6b1-fb1b-55ce-8d23-99d906744d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-uw{e}^{u+w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279671} {:answer/id #uuid "b3690af9-7cf0-5336-b7d4-e7c30102fb21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{u+v}-3{e}^{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280174} {:answer/id #uuid "f77275e3-e3d3-5b1d-ad31-9113451a2bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3w{e}^{u+v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723714f3-b594-566d-a56e-12b3c635b92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{w}(1+u)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279630} :answer-field/id #uuid "d23defb7-252c-5ea7-b5af-33ad4ec74ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10460eec-f814-5d84-b88f-d89f8f558989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161793" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}u{e}^{w} \\\\ w-u \\\\ w{e}^{u}-3v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=u{e}^{w},\\,y(u,v,w)=w-u,\\,z(u,v,w)=w{e}^{u}-3v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & ={e}^{w} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =u{e}^{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =w{e}^{u} & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-3, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & ={e}^{u}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & 0 & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ w{e}^{u} & -3 & {e}^{u}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{w} & u{e}^{w} \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3({e}^{w}+u{e}^{w}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{w}(1+u).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07db5547-d099-5a39-ad82-abc875d51fab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278662} {:answer/id #uuid "273f9725-21b0-51ad-aa3e-3681dbe69685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278942} {:answer/id #uuid "5643486d-15ec-545d-a303-f0e9cc3db1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279382} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7bc25d-f032-5290-9eff-ce5cbada276f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279426} {:answer/id #uuid "848acec7-1e41-5ca9-b95e-cefaaa4e11e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5643486d-15ec-545d-a303-f0e9cc3db1be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279382} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea688683-0e2b-5e81-8438-0d34494b3330" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281250}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27f802ec-0d96-5c3f-9520-b80524648c50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161794" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ u+3w \\\\ -v\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)={u}^{2}+{v}^{2},\\,y(u,v,w)=u+3w,\\,z(u,v,w)=-v.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =-1, & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & 2v & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(-1)^{3+2}|\\begin{aligned}2u & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6u-0 \\\\  & =6u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281328 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "555199b9-aced-5dd0-a7a4-1c3b529e0ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279370} {:answer/id #uuid "71d28527-0b0f-581a-b145-41b591816c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{uv}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279625} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf35eb4-7d06-504d-97d6-6d62c0d12660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279976} {:answer/id #uuid "9e2827ef-81bc-5319-a0af-3f83476042b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}-\\frac{1}{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279995} {:answer/id #uuid "f447c433-d789-5722-828c-42983f30e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f447c433-d789-5722-828c-42983f30e64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280731} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c51ca4-49aa-5089-b98e-4e0fed70679a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281007}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "590673d5-674e-5f87-8a3b-a26381077e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-161796" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w) \\\\ u+2w \\\\ uv\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(u\\,v\\,w),\\,y(u,v,w)=u+2w,\\,z(u,v,w)=uv.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\frac{1}{u} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\frac{1}{v} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =\\frac{1}{w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =u & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} & \\frac{1}{w} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ v & u & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}|+2(-1)^{2+3}|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} & \\frac{1}{v} \\\\ v & u\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{w}(u-0)-2(1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{u}{w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03b82058-2ed1-5ab3-8be0-f2ccd9e791be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}-vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406759} {:answer/id #uuid "2b07851c-5666-5e08-93b4-ea52710578c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} {:answer/id #uuid "4e04d287-7eaa-501d-ba5e-706a25e1adcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406764} {:answer/id #uuid "78a6574d-a7a7-5dd3-9372-265b4fd8dfa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2uv-2u{v}^{2}+vu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406765} {:answer/id #uuid "c5d7dba0-5d86-5a7b-97ed-55ed0cf1cb1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}+vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b07851c-5666-5e08-93b4-ea52710578c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406761} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc7e9e1f-8201-5457-9ae8-866cb0a50e5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eac1497-79c1-5ed3-be68-00cfa0edfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-39710" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx={u}^{2}+vw,\\,y=2-uv,\\,z={v}^{2}-w.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =2u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =v \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-u & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}2u & w & v \\\\ -v & -u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-u & 0 \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{aligned}|+(-v)(-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}w & v \\\\ 2v & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2u(u-0)+v(-w-2{v}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}-2{v}^{3}-vw.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c7d7c2-430d-5af8-9d05-fa5665e62862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} {:answer/id #uuid "6f2a26b3-3177-579e-877d-0fe20ffae536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424158} {:answer/id #uuid "9406cdae-72e2-5c66-8ad2-f1e16969ae1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424162} {:answer/id #uuid "af88273c-a488-5b6d-af48-680f8f2c6cda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424163} {:answer/id #uuid "de9aadb1-15c7-5202-b107-ef067be5cce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c7d7c2-430d-5af8-9d05-fa5665e62862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424149} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d447ea2-8560-5077-b8b9-f3304d7a91d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424173}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03a9f960-3754-59ca-a7ce-a0ebf6a90fdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-39294" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v+w,\\,y=u+w,\\,z=u+v.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+2}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|+1(-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)(0-1)+(1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146610e3-5c1c-5561-9ca3-9774666c6c05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-a-b-c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424150} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fd2ac-fcea-50a9-92dd-45cccffc9e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424155} {:answer/id #uuid "5938eea0-a7c5-5a7f-bf1f-a9fd0efaaf3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a+b+c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424157} {:answer/id #uuid "7a38c64c-896b-5232-a5e4-465265d40434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} {:answer/id #uuid "b5179334-ac94-5f42-8f5c-8c9ece45c7a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a38c64c-896b-5232-a5e4-465265d40434" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "abc" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424161} :answer-field/id #uuid "3708f8e2-7d98-51bd-8b21-20ee27c9855a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fc3f590-a25e-51e5-80e8-0d05b569e022" :question/math-academy-id "q-39664" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=au,\\,y=bv,\\,z=cw.\n$$\n\nYou may assume that $a$, $b$ and $c$ are fixed constants." :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =a & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =b & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}b & 0 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =a(bc-0) \\\\  & =abc.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of a Three-Dimensional Transformation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/14/14df7999d11afbe9002e05521d71f5008f2b49250238a714fada7dcc4aeb86d2.svg) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/00/009865419103717553af1a73b3de0ac958a76eb2fdc9c95467236581d3ad0e11.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. 